import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path('experiments/break-it-down-dag/2026-10-24-graph-first/runs/g04')
urls={
'conditional':'https://probabilitycourse.com/chapter1/1_4_0_conditional_probability.php',
'partition':'https://probabilitycourse.com/chapter1/1_4_2_total_probability.php',
'bayes':'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/naive_bayes.html',
'metrics':'https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall'}
def evidence(source,locator,claim): return {'url':urls[source],'locator':locator,'exact_claim':claim,'access':'supplied primary-source packet paraphrase; direct passage verification pending'}
def node(id,q,a,s,ev=[]): return dict(id=id,question=q,answer=a,status=s,evidence=ev)
nodes=[
node('percentages','How do I turn a percentage into a count?','Multiply the group size by the percentage divided by 100; divide a count by its group size to recover a proportion.','assumed'),
node('partition','What are the possible actual message classes?','For this model each message is either spam or legitimate, never both; the two groups cover all messages.','supported',[evidence('partition','Two-event partition formula','Disjoint exhaustive groups permit adding their contributions.')]),
node('prior','What does a 1% spam base rate describe?','Before using the flag or words, 1% of the relevant message population is spam and 99% legitimate. These are starting class proportions, or priors.','supported',[evidence('conditional','Conditional-probability definition and intuition','An unconditional probability before additional information is a prior.')]),
node('conditional','What does a probability given information mean?','Restrict attention to cases meeting the information, then find the fraction also meeting the outcome. The eligible group is the denominator; it must have positive probability.','supported',[evidence('conditional','Conditional-probability definition','P(A given B) is P(A and B) divided by P(B), for P(B)>0.')]),
node('flag_rates','Which groups do the 90% and 10% flag rates count?','90% is the share of actual spam flagged; 10% is the share of actual legitimate mail flagged. Neither percentage counts spam among all flagged messages.','supported',[evidence('metrics','Recall; False positive rate; Precision','Recall, false positive rate, and precision use different conditioning groups.')]),
node('joint_counts','How many flagged messages come from each actual class?','In an illustrative 10,000-message population, the 1% base rate gives 100 spam and 9,900 legitimate messages; the stated flag rates give 90 flagged spam and 990 flagged legitimate messages. These are expected counts under fixed rates, not a measured inbox.','partial',[evidence('conditional','Chain rule, Equation 1.5','A joint probability is a group probability multiplied by the conditional probability inside that group.')]),
node('all_flags','How many messages belong to the flagged group?','Add flagged spam and flagged legitimate messages: 90 + 990 = 1,080. Every flag is in exactly one of these groups.','partial',[evidence('partition','Law of Total Probability','Sum weighted contributions from disjoint exhaustive groups to obtain total evidence probability.')]),
node('posterior','What fraction of flagged messages is actually spam?','The desired fraction is flagged spam divided by all flags: 90/1,080 = 1/12, about 8.33%. The large legitimate population contributes eleven false flags for each true flag.','partial',[evidence('metrics','Precision','Precision is the fraction of positive predictions that are true positives.')]),
node('base_rate_change','How does a different spam base rate change that answer?','With fixed flag rates and spam fraction p, the spam fraction among flags is 0.9p / (0.9p + 0.1(1-p)). Illustrative values: p=0.1% gives about 0.89%; 1% gives 8.33%; 10% gives 50%; 50% gives 90%. Changing population composition alone changes the fraction of flags that are correct.','gap'),
node('word_clue','How can a word act like a flag?','Treat observing a word as evidence. Compare its frequency within spam with its frequency within legitimate messages, multiply each by the class prior, then normalize across the two classes as with a flag. A word is informative about spam when those within-class frequencies differ.','partial',[evidence('bayes','Opening Bayes theorem','Posterior class probability is prior times within-class evidence probability divided by evidence probability.')]),
node('training','Where do the word frequencies and priors come from?','Estimate priors from class shares in labeled training messages. For presence/absence word features, estimate how often the word occurs in messages of each class; finite samples may omit possible events, so smoothing prevents an unseen feature from forcing a class score to zero.','supported',[evidence('bayes','Opening estimation paragraph; 1.9.2; 1.9.4','Class priors and feature distributions are learned; smoothing addresses zero sample counts; Bernoulli models use binary features.')]),
node('independence','Why can naive Bayes multiply separate word-clue probabilities?','It assumes that once the class is fixed, learning the other feature values does not change the probability of a feature value. This conditional independence assumption lets the within-class probability of the observed features become a product. It is not an assertion that words in language are independent.','supported',[evidence('bayes','Opening conditional-independence equation','P(x_i given class and other features) is replaced by P(x_i given class).')]),
node('word_scores','How does naive Bayes combine several clues with a base rate?','For each class, multiply its prior by the product of the within-class probabilities for the observed feature values. Normalize the two resulting scores to obtain model probabilities. For a Bernoulli word model, an absent word is also a feature value and contributes its class-specific absence probability.','supported',[evidence('bayes','Opening classification rule; 1.9.4 Bernoulli Naive Bayes','Class score is prior times conditional-feature products; Bernoulli explicitly penalizes non-occurrence.')]),
node('probability_limit','Why might a naive Bayes score fail to describe real-world confidence?','A useful classifier can still give unreliable probability estimates. The model assumptions and estimates need evaluation on the relevant population; the stated 8.33% is a group-level calculation under stipulated rates, not a guarantee about an individual message.','partial',[evidence('bayes','Opening warning before model sections','Naive Bayes may classify well while its probability outputs are poor estimates.')]),
node('target','Why is a flag not almost certainly spam, and how do base rates and words determine the answer?','The flag rates describe separate actual classes. At 1% spam, far more legitimate messages are available to make false flags, yielding only about 8.33% spam among flags. A different base rate changes the weighted contributions. Words provide evidence by the same conditioning rule; naive Bayes estimates word-feature rates within each class and combines them under conditional independence. Its model probabilities require empirical validation.','partial')]
E=[]
def edge(a,b,why): E.append(dict(from_=a,to=b,why=why))
edge('percentages','prior','Without percentages as group shares, 1% cannot specify the starting class sizes.')
edge('partition','prior','Without complementary classes, 1% spam would not establish 99% legitimate.')
edge('percentages','conditional','Without ratios within a group, the conditioned fraction has no denominator interpretation.')
edge('conditional','flag_rates','Without identifying the conditioned group, the two given rates can be mistaken for the requested reversed rate.')
edge('prior','joint_counts','Without class sizes, within-class flag rates cannot produce population contributions.')
edge('flag_rates','joint_counts','Without the separate within-class rates, class counts cannot determine true and false flags.')
edge('partition','all_flags','Without disjoint exhaustive classes, adding the two contributions could double count or omit flags.')
edge('joint_counts','all_flags','Without both contributions, the total flag denominator is missing.')
edge('all_flags','posterior','Without the total flagged group, dividing true flags produces the wrong denominator.')
edge('conditional','posterior','Without conditioning on the observed flag, the requested fraction cannot be identified.')
edge('posterior','base_rate_change','Without the normalized true-flag share, there is no relationship whose response to the prior can be compared.')
edge('base_rate_change','word_clue','The weighted and normalized relationship is reused with word presence replacing a flag; without it the role of prior and both class likelihoods is missing.')
edge('conditional','training','Without within-class conditioning, one might count overall word frequency instead of class-specific frequencies.')
edge('conditional','independence','Without conditioning, independence within a fixed class is easily confused with unconditional word independence.')
edge('word_clue','word_scores','Without the single-clue class update, multiplying class evidence scores and then normalizing lacks its inference meaning.')
edge('independence','word_scores','Without conditional independence, the joint feature probability cannot generally be replaced by separate factors.')
edge('training','word_scores','Without learned class-specific feature probabilities and priors, the score factors have no operational values.')
edge('word_scores','probability_limit','Without knowing the score is produced by an assumed factorization, its distinction from empirically calibrated confidence is unexplained.')
edge('base_rate_change','target','Without base-rate dependence, the answer cannot explain why the same 90% detection rate gives different confidence.')
edge('word_scores','target','Without combined word-feature scores, the answer cannot connect the flag example to naive Bayes spam classification.')
edge('probability_limit','target','Without the model-versus-observed confidence limit, the answer could incorrectly treat all computed scores as reliable probabilities.')
E=[{'from':e['from_'],'to':e['to'],'why':e['why']} for e in E]
d={'title':'Bayesian spam classification: prerequisite research graph','mode':'graph-only','reader_assumptions':['Understands percentages, counts, multiplication, and division.','Has not studied probability; introduce conditioning before formulas.'],'target_id':'target','nodes':nodes,'edges':E,'scope':{'included':['Conditional versus reversed probability','Worked 1% base-rate example','Changing prevalence with fixed flag rates','Word presence as evidence','Naive Bayes combination and probability limits'],'optional_not_prerequisites':['Odds and likelihood-ratio notation','Multinomial word-count alternative','Classification thresholds and error costs','Accuracy metric','History of Bayes theorem'],'example_assumptions':['10,000 messages is a constructed counting population.','Spam and legitimate are exhaustive mutually exclusive classes.','Flag rates are stipulated and held fixed when changing base rates.','Word-model probabilities are model estimates, not guaranteed calibrated values.']},'revisions':[{'round':0,'action':'Initial graph saved before supplemental research','source_basis':'Assigned source packet only','gaps':['Verify all relevant full source passages','Verify arithmetic and dependence on base rate','Locate evidence for word training estimates, smoothing, and probability limitations'],'changes':'Initial nodes and edges as saved in history/00-initial.json'}]}
for f in ['dag.json','history/00-initial.json']: (p/f).write_text(json.dumps(d,indent=2)+'\n')
(p/'history/00-initial-notes.md').write_text('Initial state saved before supplemental browsing. Source basis: supplied packet only. Partial/gap labels retain unverified derived arithmetic, base-rate variation, word analogy, and evaluation limits. No searches yet.\n')
